# owntools landing (web/) — public configuration.
# Every variable is optional: an unset one makes the site render an honest
# "not yet" state instead of a dead link. Copy to web/.env.local to use.

# Canonical origin of the website (metadataBase, sitemap, robots, JSON-LD).
# Must be the host that actually answers 200. Production serves www and the
# apex redirects to it, so an apex value here makes every canonical, every
# sitemap entry and every e-mailed link point at a redirect.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.owntools.app

# Proves the site is yours to Google Search Console / Bing Webmaster Tools.
# Paste only the content value of the meta tag each console offers, not the
# whole tag. Needed once, to submit the sitemap and to ask for indexing.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# Switches the Windows download on. Leave unset until the first release
# ships — the download buttons then read "windows build — launching soon".
# Any installer URL will do: the buttons link to /download/windows, which
# finds the latest release's installer itself (lib/download.ts), because a
# file name with a version in it goes stale with the next release. The value
# is only used as a fallback, and only when it cannot have gone stale.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DOWNLOAD_URL_WINDOWS=

# The same switch for the macOS .dmg (universal: Apple Silicon + Intel), served
# through /download/mac. Leave unset until the first macOS release ships — the
# button then reads "mac build — launching soon" instead of pointing nowhere.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DOWNLOAD_URL_MACOS=

# --- Selling the Pro key (Polar.sh, server-side only) -------------------------
# scripts/polar-setup.ts is the whole flow: it creates the product,
# the launch-price discounts ($15 for keys 1-10, $25 for 11-30, then $39 -
# the ladder is web/lib/pricing.ts) and the benefits through the API, and
# writes LICENSE_KEY_SECRET here. Set these on the host too, then redeploy:
# the "get the pro key" button only points at /checkout when the page was
# built with a token.
#
# Organization Access Token: Polar dashboard → Settings → Developers → New
# token. Scopes: products:read/write, benefits:read/write, files:write,
# discounts:read/write, checkouts:read/write, orders:read, customers:read,
# webhooks:read/write (polar:setup then makes the webhook behind the key
# e-mail), and ideally organizations:read/write (it then checks the currency
# and sets the support e-mail; without them it says what to set by hand).
# Never NEXT_PUBLIC_ - it must not reach the browser.
POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# "sandbox" for sandbox.polar.sh (a separate account, token and test cards).
POLAR_SERVER=production
# The Polar API contract every request is pinned to (Polar-Version header).
# Unset = the default in web/lib/polarVersion.ts. Polar removes a version about
# nine months after it ships (2026-04 in the first week of January 2027); the
# scripts and the server log warn 45 days ahead.
POLAR_API_VERSION=
# Pro keys are Ed25519 signatures. The private key is derived from this and
# the public half is baked into the app (LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEY in
# packages/licensing/src/license.ts). NEVER change it once a key has been
# sold - keys signed before would stop opening the next build. polar:setup
# makes one and checks the app's constant against it; /api/license shows the
# deployed server's public half.
LICENSE_KEY_SECRET=

# --- The key by e-mail (optional; without these the key is on /thanks only) ----
# A paid order's key is e-mailed to the buyer, and owntools.app/key sends a lost
# key to the address that paid - so "I closed the tab" and "I lost my key" need
# nobody to answer them. Two parts:
#
# 1. Polar tells the site an order was paid: the `order.paid` webhook →
#    /api/polar/webhook. `pnpm polar:setup` creates the endpoint (the token
#    needs webhooks:read + webhooks:write) and writes its secret here; put the
#    same value on the host. Polar counts a redirect as a failed delivery, so
#    the script registers the address the site really answers at (www).
#    The secret is made BY POLAR (whsec_...) - it is not an access token; once
#    the host has it, polar:setup proves it with a signed test delivery.
POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#    Optional, local only: a second access token with webhooks:read/write and
#    nothing else, for polar:setup to manage the endpoint with, when the shop
#    token above has no webhook scopes. The host never needs it.
POLAR_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
# 2. Resend sends the e-mail (resend.com: add the domain, put its three DNS
#    records at the DNS host, make an API key with "Sending access"). The free
#    plan is 3,000 e-mails a month. No database anywhere: Resend's idempotency
#    keys are what keep a webhook retry from sending twice.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# The sender. Unset = "owntools <hello@owntools.app>" (the contact address); the
# domain has to be the one verified in Resend. Replies go to the contact address.
LICENSE_EMAIL_FROM=
# GET /api/polar/webhook answers which of these the deployed server has
# (booleans only) - polar:setup reads it and only lets the receipt promise an
# e-mail once all of them are there.

# Fallback before the API setup: a checkout link made by hand in the dashboard.
# Ignored once POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN is set. Unset → "checkout opens on launch day".
NEXT_PUBLIC_CHECKOUT_URL=

# Support and legal contact address (mailto: links, legal pages, refunds).
# Unset = hello@owntools.app (web/lib/site.ts); polar:setup puts the same
# address on Polar as the organization's support e-mail.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL=

# Profile on X. The "ask on x" card and the footer link render only when set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_X_URL=

# Public source repository (footer "github" link).
NEXT_PUBLIC_REPO_URL=https://github.com/SzymonnSowula/owntools

# Plausible analytics data-domain (e.g. owntools.app). Unset → no analytics script at all.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN=

# Operator shown on /privacy, /terms and /refunds: legal name and postal address.
# Unset = "OwnTools" and "Poland" (web/lib/site.ts; no company, Polar is the
# merchant of record).
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_ENTITY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_ADDRESS=

# The price is not configuration: the launch ladder lives in web/lib/pricing.ts
# and Polar keeps the counts.

# --- Share links (screeni → "Create share link") -----------------------------
# The desktop app uploads exported videos into an S3-compatible bucket
# (Cloudflare R2, MinIO, AWS S3) through presigned multipart URLs, and the
# site serves them at /v/<id>. Leave these unset and the app shows
# "sharing isn't switched on yet" instead of a broken upload.
# Step by step (R2, ~10 minutes): docs/share-links.md.
# R2 endpoint: https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, or
# https://<account-id>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com for an EU-jurisdiction bucket.
SHARE_S3_ENDPOINT=
SHARE_S3_BUCKET=
# "auto" for R2; the AWS region name for S3.
SHARE_S3_REGION=auto
# An R2 API token with "Object Read & Write" on this one bucket - nothing more.
SHARE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
SHARE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Optional public base URL of the bucket (custom domain / CDN). Without it the
# bucket stays private and /v/<id>/video.mp4 redirects to a freshly signed URL
# on every request - which is what R2 on a domain whose DNS is not at
# Cloudflare needs anyway.
SHARE_PUBLIC_BASE=
# Upload cap per video in MB (default 500) and link lifetime in days (default
# 30, 0 = keep until removed). The site stops serving a link when it expires;
# the files go only with a lifecycle rule on the bucket ("delete after
# SHARE_TTL_DAYS + 1 days") - without one they stay, and the privacy page
# (which quotes this number) would be wrong.
SHARE_MAX_MB=500
SHARE_TTL_DAYS=30
# The uploads go straight from the app to the bucket; no bucket CORS rule is
# needed because the app sends them from its native HTTP client, not the webview.
